russia's war in ukraine is having a  major impact on food supply both in and  outside of the country the united  nations food and agriculture  organization is sounding the alarm it  says one-third of ukraine's crops may be  lost this year that's about the amount  of last year's wheat crop that could go  unharvested a similar amount of corn and  sunflower is likely to go unplanted this  spring that's because much of  ukraine's farmland lies in the war zone  many farm workers have fled and there  are shortages of fuel fertilizer and  seeds  ukraine exports about 10 of the world's  wheat and 12 percent of corn much of it  goes to african countries ukraine is  also the world's biggest producer of  sunflower oil accounting for 52 percent  of all global exports in recent years  our next report in our next report we  meet a farmer in ukraine who explains  the challenges that workers like him  face in the midst of war on a reporting  trip organized by ukrainian forces dw's  matthias berlinga met a demining group  that is turning its attention to the  country's  fields the war has left its traces on  alexandre fischoen's property alexandre  is a farmer on the outskirts of kiev he  owns a thousand hectares of farmland and  agricultural machinery  but his business has been in ruins since  russian troops entered the village at  the beginning of march  a burnt-out armored vehicle sits where  his harvest is used  they came to the gate and began rattling  my workers were inside the farm  when they realized the troops were  barging through the gate they ran over  there across the fence into the village  thank god they're all alive  the russian forces installed themselves  inside the facility and alexander fled  to kiev russian troops had occupied this  place for around three weeks and when  they left  they blew up this place  now alexander is picking up the pieces  it's not just his farmhouse that was  destroyed the fighting has also affected  his farmland it is littered with  unexploded ordnance  the minesweepers haven't been here yet  so we don't dare go out we can't work at  the moment  the mining brigades are out and about  but for now they're establishing the  damage they haven't even started  demining agricultural land for this  year's harvest it might be too late the  sowing season is nearing its end  and the military administration has  little hope they will be able to help in  time  we de-mine the roads and houses first  social facilities like schools the  things that are needed so people can  more or less walk around normally we do  the fields only after we've done that  first  he guesses it might take years for  agriculture to get back to normal  meanwhile the winter wheat is growing  alexander sowed it before the war  we've flown drones over the field  and where we see traces of vehicles  my workers have been searching for mines  with metal detectors  but we need clearance for the whole  field we don't know what's lying in  there  and we'll have to wait for the mine  sweeping team to confirm that the field  is safe  the consequences of this war will  continue to be felt far and wide as  ukraine has been feeding the world for  decades  for more let's bring in joseph schmidt  huber of the un food and agriculture  organization the fao he is the deputy  director of the fao's food and markets  division thanks for joining us dr  schmidt hubba we're seeing food  shortages and rising prices around the  globe now due to the war in ukraine  tell us more about how this crisis is  impacting different world regions  yeah perhaps it's easier to look at  consumers and producers  as far as consumers are concerned  they're ubiquitously negatively affected  so they face a lot more  to spend on food and not only on food  but also another basic necessities not  to play on fuel and  fuel for heating and for cooking and for  transportation so that leaves a lot less  money for other important issues notably  for health and education of the children  and the poorest consumers are the  hottest hit  because they spend a lot more on their  incomes than the rich consumers  so most of the the hardest hits  countries are in sub-saharan africa but  they're also um countries in the nearest  north african region the nina region  that are heavily food imported  countries and and they are as a country  very strongly affected  so africa being very strongly affected  what can be done to ease these food  shortages and stabilize the supply  yeah i think it's very important to keep  food markets open so the worst thing  that can happen is  that countries start to put their feed  on the break and essentially stop  exports so export restrictions export  taxes all measures that reduce exports  are really  detrimental to international price  stability and and countries should steer  clear of these  um at the same time i think it's  important that untarget consumer  subsidies are being reduced  take egypt for instance in egypt a  particular  type of bread costs just a fraction of  what the wheat costs to produce that  bread and that leads to a lot of waste  and a lot of use as bread as animal feed  so these things are certainly not useful  we at fao we have come up with an i  think a very interesting proposal for an  international food import financing  facility so this would essentially  afford the poorest countries with high  input needs access to credit markets so  that they can they can actually buy the  food on the international market  without driving up prices when they do  that  now some analysts say that the war in  ukraine could drag on for months if not  years what should we prepare for in the  short and medium term regarding food  supplies and the price of staples  yeah it's it's difficult to say nobody  can predict prices  and i'm certainly not going to do that  but there are signs that markets will  not find back into usual equilibria as  quickly as they normally do we have this  old adage in agriculture or in commodity  markets saying essentially that  the the best cure against high prices or  high prices in the best cure against low  prices or low prices but that is not  going to happen at the moment why  because agricultural prices of food  prices are  largely determined by energy and  particularly fertilizer prices and there  is no reason no sign to assume that  fertilizer prices will come down that  essentially means as long as fertilizer  prices are high  farmers will be hesitant to use the  normal amounts of fertilizer and that  means lower production going into 2022  2023 and that is particularly pronounced  for commodities where prices are still  well behaved like for for rice so what  we call affordability fertilizer  affordability for rice is at an all-time  low meaning that farmers have no  incentive to use the normal amounts of  fertilizers and as we've seen in in sri  lanka for instance that can lead to crop  failures to very low returns and if that  is a harbinger for the rest of asia we  could actually see a full-blown food  crisis in 2022-2023  dr schmidt thank you very much for  talking with us that was jose schmidt  from the un food and agriculture  organization  thanks for having me  you
 

